4.2 Audit log correlation

When an event needs an actor identity:

- Correlate the gateway event with the relevant Discord audit-log entry where possible.
- Handle audit-log delays.
- Avoid attributing an action to the wrong administrator.
- Avoid repeatedly attributing an old audit-log entry to new events.
- Use bounded retries and appropriate correlation identifiers or timestamps.
- Distinguish an unknown actor from a confirmed actor.

Do not block urgent containment while waiting for audit-log attribution.

The security system must be able to contain a dangerous event even when the actor is not yet known.

4.3 Restore the logging destination system

Determine how logging destinations were configured in the last working version.

Restore the intended behavior for:

- Global logging configuration, if supported.
- Per-guild logging settings.
- Logging categories.
- Individual logging destinations.
- Enabled and disabled event categories.
- Webhook-based delivery.
- Fallback behavior.
- Configuration persistence across restarts.

Prevent duplicate event subscriptions and duplicate log delivery.

Ensure one event is not logged multiple times because several managers registered the same listener.

Do not silently swallow exceptions.

Logging failures should be visible to developers without creating an infinite logging-error loop.

4.4 Logging must reflect reality

Every log message must describe an event or action that actually occurred.

For example:

- Do not report a channel as deleted if deletion failed.
- Do not report a permission as changed if the API request failed.
- Do not report lockdown as successful if no restrictions were applied.
- Do not report a webhook as created until creation succeeds.
- Do not claim a log was delivered when Discord rejected the request.

Use accurate statuses and clear failure messages.

